---
import BaseLayout from '@layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '@components/PageHero.astro';

const sections = [
  {
    href: '/en/linguistique/methode-et-invariants',
    eyebrow: 'Method',
    title: 'Method and PT invariants',
    summary: 'Inter-occurrence character gap extraction, T_m matrices, δ(m) spectroscopy, q⁺/q⁻ bifurcation, axioms P1–P7. S1–S11 table: 6/10 invariants survive (algebraic core), 4/10 fail (prime-specific arithmetic periphery).',
    tag: 'Scientific core',
  },
  {
    href: '/en/linguistique/diachronie-et-evolvabilite',
    eyebrow: 'Dynamics',
    title: 'Diachrony and evolvability',
    summary: 'PT evolvability (E_vol = χ·I·reclosure / (1+τ_norm)) applied to three Caribbean creoles (2019/2021/2023) and to ancient → modern pairs (Old French → French, Old English → English). Corpus confound caveat acknowledged.',
    tag: 'Evolution',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="PT-Linguistics programme"
  description="Crash-test of the PT core on natural language. Method (S1–S11 invariants, mixture-of-geometrics, q⁺/q⁻ bifurcation), benchmarks (Voynich, Linear A), diachrony (creoles, ancient vs modern) and client-side interactive tools."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Exploratory programme"
    domain="language"
    title="PT-Linguistics"
    kicker="Testing the PT core on a domain with no parity bias, no sieve, Zipfian statistics."
    dek="If the cascade {3, 5, 7} and the q⁺/q⁻ bifurcation are generic, natural language must carry their trace. This programme does not aim to “decipher” the Voynich or Linear A; it uses these corpora — and 12 living or dead languages — as stringent benchmarks for the algebraic core of PT. Results are pipeline outputs, conditional on PT, and do not commit the philology community."
    oneLineNote={{ label: 'Status', body: 'exploratory programme. Code in release, article in preparation. These results do not belong to PT’s main deductive chain (informational conservation → sieve dynamics → crystallisation → attractor μ* = 15 → observables); they are cumulative transpositions, to be confirmed by domain specialists.' }}
    width="wide"
  />

  <main class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 py-10 md:py-14">

    <section class="mb-12">
      <p class="pt-tag pt-tag-thm mb-3">Two public pages</p>
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-thmblue mb-6">Method and dynamics</h2>
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        {sections.map((s) => (
          <a href={s.href} class="block rounded-lg border-2 border-cyan-300 bg-cyan-50 p-6 transition hover:shadow-lg hover:bg-cyan-100/60">
            <div class="flex items-baseline gap-2 mb-2">
              <span class="text-xs font-sans uppercase tracking-wider text-cyan-700 font-semibold">{s.eyebrow}</span>
              <span class="text-xs font-sans text-stone-500">· {s.tag}</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="font-sans text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">{s.title}</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">{s.summary}</p>
            <p class="mt-3 font-sans text-sm font-semibold text-cyan-700">→ Open</p>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="mt-6 rounded-lg border border-stone-300 bg-stone-50 p-5">
        <p class="text-xs font-sans uppercase tracking-wider text-stone-500 mb-2">Internal exploratory notes</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
          The pipeline has been applied to two undeciphered scripts (the
          Voynich manuscript, the Linear A syllabary) as stringent
          benchmarks. Outputs are preliminary, depend on reference corpus
          choices, and call for validation by specialist philologists —
          they are not exposed publicly at this stage. Repository and
          notes available to interested researchers via
          <a href="/en/contact" class="text-thmblue underline">contact</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <p class="pt-tag pt-tag-id mb-3">Why language</p>
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-thmblue mb-4">A stringent falsification ground</h2>
      <div class="rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
        <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          PT was born on prime number gaps: a domain with a parity bias
          (gaps are nearly always even), a sieve structure, and strong
          arithmetic regularity. The risk for such a theory is to
          <em>extract its invariants from the specifics of the domain</em>
          and say nothing elsewhere.
        </p>
        <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Language offers the opposite:
        </p>
        <ul class="space-y-3 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
          <li><strong class="text-cyan-700">No parity bias</strong> — character inter-occurrence gaps are arbitrary.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-cyan-700">No sieve</strong> — no arithmetic elimination mechanism structures positions.</li>
          <li><strong class="text-cyan-700">Zipfian statistics</strong> — frequency distributions follow a power law, very different from a uniform arithmetic distribution.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mt-4">
          What survives of the PT core under these conditions is what is
          algebraic by nature (transition matrices, GFT identity,
          Cencov–Fisher, q⁺/q⁻). What breaks is what depended specifically
          on the sieve. This is the
          <strong>algebraic core</strong> /
          <strong>arithmetic periphery</strong> separation that this
          programme makes measurable.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <p class="pt-tag pt-tag-id mb-3">Honesty</p>
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-thmblue mb-4">What this programme is NOT</h2>
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        <div class="rounded-lg border-2 border-rose-300 bg-rose-50 p-5">
          <h4 class="font-sans font-semibold text-rose-800 mb-2">✗ Not a decipherment programme</h4>
          <p class="text-sm text-rose-900 leading-relaxed">
            Voynich and Linear A are used as <em>benchmarks</em> for the
            PT robustness hierarchy (L1 ≥ L2 ≥ L3), not as decipherment
            projects. Pipeline outputs are candidates that specialist
            philologists must validate or refute.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border-2 border-rose-300 bg-rose-50 p-5">
          <h4 class="font-sans font-semibold text-rose-800 mb-2">✗ Not a linguistic theory</h4>
          <p class="text-sm text-rose-900 leading-relaxed">
            No claim about what language is. This programme measures
            statistical invariants on character or syllable sequences and
            compares them with PT predictions.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border-2 border-rose-300 bg-rose-50 p-5">
          <h4 class="font-sans font-semibold text-rose-800 mb-2">✗ Not a [THM] theorem</h4>
          <p class="text-sm text-rose-900 leading-relaxed">
            S1–S11 invariants are empirical tests, not theorems derived
            from s = 1/2. The PT → language transposition is an empirically
            validated analogy, not a rigorous derivation.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-lg border-2 border-rose-300 bg-rose-50 p-5">
          <h4 class="font-sans font-semibold text-rose-800 mb-2">✗ Not PT’s central principle</h4>
          <p class="text-sm text-rose-900 leading-relaxed">
            PT’s central principle — under constraint, part disperses as
            entropy and part persists as structure compatible with the
            filter’s logic — first unfolds as a deductive chain
            (informational conservation → sieve dynamics →
            crystallisation of 2 → attractor μ* = 15 → observables).
            This linguistic programme is an <em>empirical
            transposition</em> of that chain to a non-arithmetic domain:
            it tests its reach, it does not replace it (cf.
            <a href="/en/premier-principe" class="underline">fundamental principle</a>).
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8">
      <p class="pt-tag pt-tag-bridge mb-3">Going further</p>
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-thmblue mb-6">Links</h2>
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
        <a href="/en/recherches" class="pt-card block group">
          <span class="pt-tag pt-tag-thm">Research</span>
          <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-thmblue text-lg mt-3 mb-2 group-hover:underline">All three public programmes</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-700">Biology, colour perception, linguistics: three transpositions of the PT cascade.</p>
        </a>
        <a href="/en/principes-architecturaux" class="pt-card block group">
          <span class="pt-tag pt-tag-thm">Meta-theory</span>
          <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-thmblue text-lg mt-3 mb-2 group-hover:underline">7 architectural principles</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-700">Why the same patterns recur across very different domains.</p>
        </a>
        <a href="/en/critique" class="pt-card block group">
          <span class="pt-tag pt-tag-id">Critique</span>
          <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-thmblue text-lg mt-3 mb-2 group-hover:underline">Discuss these results</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-700">Objections, counter-tests, code and corpus access requests.</p>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

  </main>
</BaseLayout>
